      ":question/id": "eff323d1-4e70-5490-8e41-9fbf00c387f5",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-446",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int (3x+1)^3 \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3x+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 4(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(3x+1)^{3}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
