        ":question/id": "b151b1a4-88b3-56e5-8da5-20d1f21f5f65",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113492",
        ":question/problem": "$\\int 6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=5-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(5-3x)^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{3}(5-3x)^{3}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{3}(5-3x)^{3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(5-3x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-2(5-3x)^{2}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
