        ":question/problem": "$\\int 9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=3-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{u}^{8}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\int {u}^{8}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\int {u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{9}}{9}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{9}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3-2x)^{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{2}(3-2x)^{9}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}(3-2x)^{9}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(3-2x)^{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3-2x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(3-2x)^{8}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
